WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.874
Welcome to Module Two: Agent Development.

2
00:00:03.204 --> 00:00:07.335
This is where designs become working code, and where agents quietly break.

3
00:00:08.135 --> 00:00:11.029
Picture a travel startup launching a booking assistant.

4
00:00:11.359 --> 00:00:14.214
It searches flights, checks hotels, and books trips,

5
00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.280
all through tools the model can call.

6
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.041
Its tools were written in a hurry, each with one vague line of description.

7
00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:25.481
So a customer asks for a Lisbon hotel, and the model calls flight search.

8
00:00:26.281 --> 00:00:29.281
Even with the right tool, the model guesses the arguments.

9
00:00:29.611 --> 00:00:34.404
It sends the date as next Friday-ish, and the system just replies: bad request.

10
00:00:35.204 --> 00:00:38.183
The app needs clean JSON to draw the booking screen.

11
00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:42.538
But sometimes the model wraps it in friendly chatter, and the page crashes.

12
00:00:43.338 --> 00:00:47.427
Every hotel search dumps pages of raw data into the conversation.

13
00:00:47.757 --> 00:00:51.227
By step eight, the assistant has forgotten the customer's budget.

14
00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:54.686
Then a booking times out, and the agent tries again.

15
00:00:55.016 --> 00:00:57.803
But the first one went through, so a family pays twice.

16
00:00:58.603 --> 00:01:01.135
The team's only test checks for one exact sentence.

17
00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:04.401
Any harmless rewording breaks it, so nobody trusts it.

18
00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:06.773
The fix wasn't a bigger model.

19
00:01:07.103 --> 00:01:10.487
It was treating tools, outputs, and context as contracts.

20
00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:12.847
Lesson one is tool design:

21
00:01:12.897 --> 00:01:16.933
descriptions that say when not to use a tool, plus strict schemas.

22
00:01:17.263 --> 00:01:21.203
And idempotency keys make a retried booking charge only once.

23
00:01:22.003 --> 00:01:23.979
Lesson two is structured output.

24
00:01:24.309 --> 00:01:26.853
Asking for JSON makes errors unlikely,

25
00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:30.745
but constrained decoding makes invalid output impossible.

26
00:01:31.545 --> 00:01:33.477
Lesson three maps the frameworks,

27
00:01:33.527 --> 00:01:37.383
like LangGraph for stateful agents that pause for human approval.

28
00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:39.688
And NVIDIA's NeMo Agent Toolkit,

29
00:01:39.738 --> 00:01:43.295
which wraps them with profiling, evaluation, and tracing.

30
00:01:44.095 --> 00:01:46.125
Lesson four is context engineering:

31
00:01:46.175 --> 00:01:49.037
deciding what belongs in the window at each step.

32
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:51.297
Raw results become short digests,

33
00:01:51.347 --> 00:01:54.778
and a stable prompt prefix keeps each step fast and cheap.

34
00:01:55.578 --> 00:01:58.417
Lesson five tests properties, not exact strings:

35
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:01.245
the right tool, valid arguments, within budget.

36
00:02:01.575 --> 00:02:05.280
Each case runs several times, and traces show which stage failed.

37
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.055
This domain is fifteen percent of the exam, and its favourite trap:

38
00:02:10.105 --> 00:02:13.539
a schema guarantees a well-formed call, not a correct one.

39
00:02:13.869 --> 00:02:16.550
Lesson one starts right there, with tool design.
